This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.

Summary:
1. Primary Request and Intent:
   Senior .NET/Keycloak/distributed-systems task to extend existing Identity microservice solution with dynamic role management and Keycloak-backed scoped access control while PRESERVING existing permission synchronization flow:
   ```
   Microservice starts -> declares/registers permissions -> sends to Identity Facade -> Facade synchronizes to Keycloak
   ```
   Non-negotiable: do NOT redesign sync, new permissions must use same mechanism, Facade remains sole Keycloak abstraction. Goals: Keycloak source of truth for users/credentials/roles/permission roles/composite business roles/assignments/scopes; no second SQL/EF auth DB; no duplication unless existing architecture requires. Permission model: atomic permissions remain Keycloak realm roles e.g. orders.read, synchronized idempotently; each microservice declares own. Dynamic business roles: dynamically creatable/editable/removable via Facade e.g. SuperAdmin, RegionalManager, NOT hardcoded enums, prefer Keycloak Composite Roles (business role composites atomic permissions), operations: create/rename, assign/remove permissions, read effective, delete safely, list. Dynamic scoped access: arbitrary future scope dimensions (region, branch, etc.) without schema migration, model as Dictionary<string,IReadOnlyCollection<string>>, no fixed columns. Scoped role assignment must preserve Role->Scopes association (ScopedRoleAssignment{Role, Scopes}), never flatten. Keycloak storage: single namespaced attribute iam.scoped_access JSON array of assignments, centralized IScopedAccessStore typed<->serializer<->adapter, merge only owned attribute preserving unrelated attrs, optional built-in protocol mapper to expose as iam_access claim, no custom SPI unless necessary. Token/claims: expose identity/effective permissions/scoped assignments via compact iam_access claim, centralized ICurrentAccessContext with GetAssignments/HasPermission(scopeKey,scopeValue)/GetScopeValues, must originate from Keycloak identity, consider JWT size guard fallback to facade resolution, avoid distributed cache unless existing. User Management API following existing REST/versioning/Result/validation conventions: Create/Update/Get/List users, Assign/Update/Remove/Get scoped assignments, Create/Update/Delete/List business roles, Assign/Remove/Get effective permissions, List permissions; validate roles/permissions, idempotent, handle partial failures with compensation. Management permissions: identity.users.read/manage, identity.roles.read/manage, identity.scopes.read/manage registered via existing startup mechanism. Using scopes in microservices: provide reusable primitives like accessContext.GetScopeValues("region") for query.Where(allowed.Contains(x.Region)) and HasPermission(...,scopeKey,scopeValue), no reflection-heavy EF filter, filtering in DB. Security: frontend checks UX only, backend must check permission+scope. Scalar/OpenAPI: preserve Scalar, new service appears in same v1 document aggregator, Bearer scheme, grouping consistent. Configuration: follow Options pattern, no hardcode realm/URLs/secrets/attr names, sensible constants for iam.scoped_access/iam_access. Code quality: small extensions, async/CancellationToken/nullable, existing DI/HttpClient/resilience/logging, avoid duplicate clients/scattered calls/magic strings, keep Keycloak behind facade. Tests follow existing style covering 18 scenarios. Implementation strategy incremental 13 steps via plan file phases. Final deliverable must provide concise summary containing projects/files changed, new endpoints, Keycloak representation, attribute/claim names, business role representation, service consumption, Scalar integration, Keycloak config migration, tests results, limitations.

